dmenu - dynamic menu
====================
dmenu is an efficient dynamic menu for X.


Requirements
------------
Building requires a C99 compiler, make, and development headers/libraries for
Xlib, Xft, Fontconfig and FreeType; the default build also uses Xinerama.
The linker uses libm. Disable both Xinerama settings in config.mk if unwanted.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dmenu is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Normal `make install` builds and installs dmenu, stest, dmenu_path, dmenu_run
and clipmenu-preview under `$(PREFIX)/bin`, with mode 0755, plus the dmenu
and stest manuals under `$(MANPREFIX)/man1`. MANPREFIX defaults to
`$(PREFIX)/share/man`. `make uninstall` removes those same files; it does
not restore overwritten versions or remove unrelated files.

For a separately authorized user-local installation and matching removal:

    make install PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    make uninstall PREFIX="$HOME/.local"

These are alternatives, not a command sequence to run together. Back up any
existing destination files before installation. No host installation is
authorized by development verification. PREFIX need not be changed in config.mk.
For packaging, DESTDIR prepends a staging root to both executable and manual
destinations; in a disposable source copy, for example:

    make install DESTDIR=/absolute/private/stage PREFIX=/usr
    make uninstall DESTDIR=/absolute/private/stage PREFIX=/usr

This maps executables to `/absolute/private/stage/usr/bin` and manuals to
`/absolute/private/stage/usr/share/man/man1`. If MANPREFIX is overridden, pass
the same override to uninstall. `make dist` continues shipping the helper once.


Running dmenu
-------------
See the man page for details.


Text preview (development only)
-------------------------------
`-P /absolute/helper -l 12` or `-P clipmenu-preview -l 12` runs a trusted
executable directly and draws its text in the approved 40/60 split below the
search input. The helper receives the unchanged selected label plus LF through
stdin, then EOF. Its argv has only the executable path; stdout is preview data
and stderr goes to /dev/null.
No clipboard text is evaluated as a command or added to the environment.
An explicit positive decimal `-l` is required; preview mode is never enabled
without `-P`.

A nonempty name without a slash is resolved in the forked helper child on
each request, using inherited PATH from left to right. Missing, non-regular
and non-executable candidates are skipped; the first executable regular file
(including a symlink to one) is selected. Empty PATH components mean the inherited
working directory; relative components are relative to that directory. Unset
PATH supplies no candidates; explicitly empty PATH searches only the working
directory. Absolute helper paths bypass PATH; relative paths containing slashes
such as `./helper` are rejected.

Execution uses execv, never shell evaluation or an ENOEXEC-to-shell fallback.
After a candidate is selected, execution failure does not try later candidates.
Missing helpers and failed execution show Unavailable, preserving selection and
cancellation. Trust all searched directories, the working directory when searched,
and helper code. Checks are not atomic against filesystem changes; this is not
a sandbox. A valid executable format or interpreter header is required.

One request owns a helper process group. Selection changes cancel obsolete
work and debounce for 40 ms. A 1000 ms monotonic deadline covers execution,
stdin delivery, stdout collection and process completion. Output is capped
at 65536 bytes; an observed extra byte fixes the result as Truncated even
after producer termination. Exact-limit successful completion is not truncated.
The other fixed states are Loading, No preview, Unavailable and Timed out.
Successful output waits for EOF and exit 0; ordinary nonzero exit discards it.

Pipe I/O, poll-based X11 servicing and reaping are nonblocking. SIGKILL is sent
to the owned group before releasing its leader PID; process disappearance is
eventual, not an exit-time wait. Intentionally escaped descendants are outside
containment guarantees. This is not a sandbox for hostile helper executables.
Without `-P`, dmenu still blocks in XNextEvent without preview allocations,
children or periodic polling.

In preview mode, overflowing list labels ending in clipmenu's exact
` (N lines)` suffix (ASCII decimal N >= 2, no leading zero) retain the count
at the right padded edge. Only the preceding text is shortened. Fitting
labels, other labels and all no-preview rendering remain unchanged. The
original label is never edited: filtering, helper stdin/checksum lookup and
selection stdout still use every original byte. At widths too small for the
count, it is clipped inside the item; padding-only widths may be blank.

The last body row is reserved for status; preceding rows display a scrollable
viewport of soft-wrapped text. With one body row, Ready uses that row; other
states display status. In particular, `-l 1` with Truncated remains status-only:
use at least two body rows to read a truncated result. LF preserves empty and
trailing rows. CR (including CRLF's CR), NUL, ESC, remaining C0/C1 controls,
DEL and invalid UTF-8 bytes become U+FFFD; terminal escapes are not interpreted.
Tabs expand to four codepoint-column stops, reset on LF but not soft wraps.
Valid UTF-8 is retained. Wrapping uses the active Xft scalar advances, never
splits a codepoint, and clips glyph ink to each preview row. Grapheme clusters,
complex shaping and missing-font glyph coverage retain existing Xft limitations.

Preparation processes at most 256 codepoints per event-loop turn, counting
expanded tab spaces as work. Loading remains visible until layout is complete;
selection changes discard pending layout. The helper deadline ends at completed
collection, not layout. Raw storage remains 65537 bytes; display storage is at
most 4 * retained raw bytes + 1, and row offsets have at most that many size_t
entries (262145 display bytes and 262145 entries at the raw cap). These separate
allocation ceilings include sanitization expansion and narrow-pane wrapping.
Only pending preparation uses zero-timeout polling.

Alt-Up/Down scroll one prepared visual row; Alt-PageUp/PageDown scroll one
text viewport; Alt-Home/End move to the first/last viewport. The offset clamps
to `max(0, prepared rows - viewport rows)`, including blank/trailing rows and
soft wraps. Scrolling never reruns the helper or preparation, changes selection,
edits input, or emits stdout. Only an actual selected-item change resets it;
cursor movement, Expose and same-selection redraws retain it. Pending/loading
or empty results do not scroll. Ctrl shortcuts and existing Alt-letter/list
navigation retain their original behavior.

The standalone `clipmenu-preview` adapter is included in the source distribution
and normal `make install`/`make uninstall`. Do not replace a working installed
dmenu or enable this integration against real history without separate
deployment authorization.

The adapter accepts no arguments. It reads the original display label plus LF
from stdin with `IFS= read -r`, then streams the cached payload unchanged,
including binary bytes and trailing newlines. It uses unset-only
`${CM_DIR-${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR-${TMPDIR-/tmp}}}` defaults; an explicitly empty
base means `/clipmenu.6.$USER`, not a fallback. USER must contain only ASCII
letters, digits, `_`, `.`, or `-`, and cannot be empty, `.` or `..`. The filename
is the complete `cksum` output for the exact label plus one LF: CRC, a space,
and byte count. It does not regenerate labels or read `line_cache`.

After an explicitly authorized installation, the intended Xmonad command is:

    clipmenu -i -p Clipboard -P clipmenu-preview -l 12

Both the intended dmenu executable and helper must be discoverable in the actual
environment inherited by Xmonad and its launched clipmenu/dmenu process.
`$HOME/.local/bin` is not automatically visible to Xmonad. Ensure its inherited
PATH includes the chosen installation bin directory, in the intended order.
If clipmenu's CM_LAUNCHER is overridden, it must identify the intended dmenu.
No working Xmonad configuration is changed by these instructions.

An absolute-path development invocation remains supported:

    CM_LAUNCHER=/home/avo/Projects/private/dmenu-preview-next/dmenu \
      clipmenu -i -p Clipboard \
      -P /home/avo/Projects/private/dmenu-preview-next/clipmenu-preview -l 12

`CM_LAUNCHER` is one executable path, not a command string. clipmenu initially
adds `-l "$CM_HISTLENGTH"` (default 8), then forwards the command-line arguments
unchanged; the final `-l 12` overrides the initial value. The selected label
continues back through clipmenu's existing restoration path. The adapter only
reads the cache; it never writes history or clipboard/PRIMARY and dmenu's output
cap applies only to preview display, not restoration.

The final cache directory must be nonsymlink, current-UID-owned and have no
group/other permissions. Payloads must be readable regular nonsymlink files.
GNU `stat`, `id`, `cksum` and `cat` are used; there are no new dependencies.
Detected pre-stream errors return nonzero with no payload or diagnostics.
Duplicate labels overwrite the same checksum-named cache file, and checksum
collisions are not disambiguated: the adapter reads whichever current file
clipmenu's lookup names. Cache retention can remove a payload. Ancestor
directories, PATH tools and same-UID peers are trusted. Shell path checks are
not atomic against concurrent mutation; concurrent rewrites can expose changing
or partial bytes, and streaming I/O failure can expose a prefix. No locking,
snapshot consistency, sandboxing or stronger identity guarantee is provided.

Focused install and literal helper-lookup regressions:

    python3 tests/preview-install.py
    python3 -B tests/preview-path.py \
        --binary /absolute/disposable/current/dmenu \
        --baseline /absolute/disposable/baseline/dmenu \
        --xvfb /absolute/Xvfb --output /absolute/empty/results

The install regression builds, stages install/uninstall and runs dist only in
a disposable source copy, preserving local config.h. The lookup regression
starts its own Xvfb and uses synthetic helpers, controlled PATH/cwd, exact bytes
and screenshots; it never runs clipmenu or touches clipboard/history. Build
both supplied binaries in disposable copies with the same local configuration;
the baseline is the starting build. Output must be empty. `-B` avoids creating
Python import caches in the checkout.

Focused synthetic verification (does not run clipmenu or any X client):

    sh -n clipmenu-preview
    python3 tests/clipmenu-preview.py

The regression uses private temporary fixtures, with a unique synthetic USER
and exclusive mkdir for its `/tmp` fallback cache. It compares Bash here-string
checksums, byte-exact payloads, defaults and filesystem rejection boundaries,
checking fixture contents/permissions around each invocation. Foreign ownership
uses optional `fakeroot` metadata simulation only for non-root runs. That case
is skipped if fakeroot is unavailable or EUID is zero: fakeroot may allow a
real privileged chown when run as root. Root also skips the unreadable-file
case because root can read mode 000.

The launcher/restoration regression owns its Xvfb and uses a synthetic cache,
fake `xsel`, and isolated PATH. Run it with an absolute installed launcher,
disposable dmenu binary, Xvfb executable and empty output directory:

    python3 tests/clipmenu-launcher.py \
        --launcher /home/avo/.guix-profile/bin/clipmenu \
        --binary /absolute/disposable/dmenu \
        --xvfb /tmp/dmenu-preview-checkpoint-RBbWFs/xvfb/usr/bin/Xvfb \
        --output /absolute/empty/results

The test invokes `/usr/bin/bash /home/avo/.guix-profile/bin/clipmenu` so Bash
runs the installed wrapper's body directly, bypassing the Guix Guile wrapper
and its PATH changes without modifying the installed launcher. It must never
execute the wrapper normally, start the daemon, invoke real `xsel`, or access
real history/clipboard. It compares both fake clipboard and PRIMARY sink bytes
with the original multiline fixture payload, and verifies the selected display
label is unchanged. Checkpoint 5B results are recorded in OpenSpec tasks.md.

Installation is a later, separately authorized action—not current deployment
authorization. Before normal installation, record PREFIX, MANPREFIX and every
destination's prior existence, and back up all existing executables and manuals
that the target will replace, including clipmenu-preview. Preserve those files
with `cp -Pp` in a recorded backup directory; stop if a backup fails. Do not
overwrite a symlink destination without separately approving its actual target.
Record the prior CM_LAUNCHER executable and forwarded arguments as well.

Rollback first restores the recorded previous launcher invocation without `-P`,
then restores each saved destination with `cp -Pp`. Remove only files recorded as
absent before installation. `make uninstall` removes the full installed set at
the supplied PREFIX/MANPREFIX; it is not a backup-restoration operation. Neither
installation nor rollback requires a daemon restart or history migration.
No install, replacement or real-history invocation is authorized here.

The Checkpoint 5B integration smoke and fixture restoration evidence belong to
the current OpenSpec change. Do not treat them as completion of the full
group-6 suite or authorization to deploy.

Build and test only disposable source copies, preserving configuration and
existing binaries/objects:

    work=$(mktemp -d /tmp/dmenu-preview-async.XXXXXX)
    cp Makefile config.mk *.c config.def.h arg.h drw.h util.h "$work/"
    make -C "$work" config.h
    make -C "$work" CC='cc -Wextra'
    cat > "$work/preview" <<'EOF'
    #!/bin/sh
    IFS= read -r label || exit 1
    printf 'Synthetic preview\n\nSelected label:\n%s\n' "$label"
    EOF
    chmod 700 "$work/preview"

Remove the four spaces of Markdown indentation when entering the heredoc.
Use only a disposable Xvfb/Xephyr display, never the working desktop:

    printf 'alpha\nbeta\ngamma\n' |
        DISPLAY=:ISOLATED "$work/dmenu" -i -l 12 -p Preview -P "$work/preview"

Replace `:ISOLATED` with the display allocated by that isolated server. Enter,
Shift-Return, Ctrl-Return and Escape retain the existing output/exit contracts.
No installation or PATH modification is needed.

`python3 tests/preview-checkpoint.py --baseline /absolute/baseline/dmenu
--binary /absolute/new/dmenu --output /tmp/preview-results --isolated-display`
(one command, with DISPLAY set to the owned isolated server) requires an empty
output directory. It confirms at least 200 actual helper starts, measures active
and settled resources, samples intermediate stale-result frames, and exercises
deadline/navigation/cancellation under bounded Expose traffic. Per-process stderr
and cleanup records are retained in OUTPUT/diagnostics, including unexpected
termination and forced cleanup. Sanitizer diagnostics fail the run; partial
reports are retained on failure. See `--help` for focused sanitizer runs.
Xvfb/Xephyr, xdotool, xwininfo and ImageMagick are verification tools, not
application dependencies.

The older highlight check assumes default colors; row positions come from
actual geometry. Preview-content checks use same-run pixel variation rather
than fixed glyph hashes. Use matching baseline/current configuration and fonts,
not custom themes, for the screenshot comparisons.

Approved earlier GUI screenshots and logs remain unchanged under
`openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-28-add-text-preview/evidence/`. Their compile-time fixture and
checkpoint notice have been removed from the application; synthetic content
now comes exclusively from executable test helpers.

Async-checkpoint source delta, screenshots and executable verification results
are retained under `openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-28-add-text-preview/evidence/async/`.
Post-review harness corrections and fresh verification evidence are retained
under `openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-28-add-text-preview/evidence/async-review/`.
Those directories retain historical sanitizer findings from before integration
of reviewed fix `32fafa2ce7daf1a683e3039bbaf7a9007823e986`: fuzzy filtering
read one pointer beyond the sorted match array. The bounds-first loop correction
is now integrated; the fuzzy algorithm and token-mode behavior are unchanged.
Fontconfig exit leaks remain unrelated and unfixed.
Fresh integration commands, outcomes, diagnostics and preservation checks are
under `openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-28-add-text-preview/evidence/fuzzymatch-integration/`.

Byte-exact collection checks use `tests/preview-boundary.c`, a test-only
translation unit including the real private lifecycle functions from dmenu.c.
It checks retained bytes/length/terminator/canary, terminal precedence and
stdout-EOF timeout without opening X11. Build it in a disposable source copy:

    mkdir "$work/tests"
    cp tests/preview-boundary.c "$work/tests/"
    make -C "$work" -f Makefile -f - CC='cc -Wextra' preview-boundary <<'EOF'
    preview-boundary: tests/preview-boundary.c dmenu.c config.h drw.o util.o
    	$(CC) $(CFLAGS) -o $@ $< drw.o util.o $(LDFLAGS)
    EOF
    PREVIEW_TEST_HELPER="$work/preview-boundary" "$work/preview-boundary"

Use separate fresh copies for normal, UBSan and ASan builds; sanitizer compiler
flags must be present during compilation and linking. PREVIEW_TEST_HELPER must
always name the normal, uninstrumented probe, even for a sanitized parent: the
production protocol discards helper stderr. Parent diagnostics remain captured.
The native probe can run with leak detection enabled.

Safe-text regression checks use `tests/preview-text.c`, which includes the
production scanner/layout with deterministic font advances and an explicit
256-codepoint progress check. Build it like preview-boundary, substituting
preview-text for the target and source name. Run with
`PREVIEW_TEXT_HELPER=/absolute/normal/preview-text /absolute/build/preview-text`;
the producer must be the normal uninstrumented probe even for sanitized parents.
Native leak detection stays enabled. This checks a real completed helper whose
deliberately slow preparation finishes beyond the helper deadline.
The same native probe checks visual-row scroll bounds, page increments, blank
and trailing rows, selection reset, and zero font-metric calls during scrolling.
Build `tests/preview-clip.c` the same way with target `preview-clip`. It uses
real Liberation Mono:size=8 and DejaVu Sans:size=48 on a 24-bit isolated display,
comparing a 70-pixel overwide glyph in a 40x16 region against an independent
clipped Xft reference, ellipsis and blank pixels. It also checks a preceding
font run, ordinary ellipsis and isolation from subsequent drawing calls.

On an owned isolated X11 server, run:

    python3 tests/preview-text.py --binary /absolute/build/dmenu \
        --layout-probe /absolute/build/preview-text \
        --clip-probe /absolute/build/preview-clip \
        --output /tmp/text-results --isolated-display

Use an empty output directory. Synthetic helpers cover Unicode, malformed/control
bytes, wrapping, narrow panes and alternate fonts; screenshots and process stderr
are retained. The test-only layout probe delays font measurement to make pending
preparation and cancellation observable without a production hook or option.
Focused scrolling and input-compatibility checks reuse the synthetic X11
infrastructure and require the already installed `xclip` only as an isolated
PRIMARY selection owner for paste tests:

    python3 tests/preview-scroll.py --binary /absolute/build/dmenu \
        --baseline /absolute/baseline/dmenu \
        --output /tmp/scroll-results --isolated-display

Set DISPLAY only to an owned disposable server and use an empty output directory.
The harness compares exact row shifts and viewport pixels, helper-start counts,
selection reset, a prepared 65520-byte preview, and baseline/current navigation
in fuzzy/token and case-sensitive/insensitive modes. It retains process stderr;
GUI ASan runs use `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0` for the accepted Fontconfig limit,
separately from leak-enabled native probes and prior leak-enabled diagnostics.

Focused multiline-label rendering checks build `tests/preview-label.c` like
the production-include probes above, substituting `preview-label` for the
target/source name. Copy both new test files plus `preview-scroll.py` into
the disposable source copy. Then run:

    python3 tests/preview-label.py --binary /absolute/build/dmenu \
        --baseline /absolute/starting-build/dmenu \
        --probe /absolute/build/preview-label --xvfb /absolute/Xvfb \
        --output /absolute/empty/label-results

This driver owns its X server and uses synthetic labels/payloads only.
It checks real-Xft suffix pixels, item confinement at narrow widths,
fitting/unrecognized-label compatibility, highlighting schemes, complete
helper stdin and Return stdout, and no-preview baseline pixels. It retains
screenshots and stderr without repeating the complete lifecycle/GUI suites.
This focused check does not complete OpenSpec tasks 6.4 or 6.5.


The focused real-Xft responsiveness benchmark builds instrumented probes in
disposable source copies and starts its own Xvfb:

    python3 tests/preview-fonts.py --short \
        --baseline-source /absolute/starting-4a-copy \
        --corrected-source /absolute/current-copy \
        --xvfb /absolute/Xvfb --output /tmp/font-results

Both copies must have `config.h`; the driver copies the current test probe into
their `tests/` directories. Short mode measures 1/8 KiB varied Unicode inputs,
three Escape/Return repetitions each, and complete preparation with per-step
and per-glyph timings. `samples.json` and `complete-preparation.json` retain
raw results and actual widths. Only measurements inside preparation steps
participate in width comparisons. Baseline latency is observational, not a
requirement that every injected event exceed 100 ms. The 4 ms preparation
budget yields between glyphs; it cannot interrupt a synchronous Xft lookup.

Final acceptance evidence and limits are indexed in the Checkpoint 6E section
of `openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-28-add-text-preview/tasks.md`. Automated regressions,
sanitizer/resource measurements, staged packaging/rollback and human-confirmed
Xmonad observations are separate evidence categories, not deployment approval.
The Fontconfig/Xft GUI exit leak remains; reduced-quarantine ASan measurements
do not establish default-quarantine RSS stability. The accepted Checkpoint 6B
DISPLAY protocol exception remains disclosed there. Valgrind was unavailable;
OpenBSD runtime and other physical-monitor configurations remain untested.

For focused ASan lifecycle checks in both fuzzy and token modes,
`ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0` isolates invalid accesses from the known Fontconfig
exit leak; address checks remain enabled and this is not a leak-clean claim.
Run separate baseline/current normal-exit checks with `detect_leaks=1` and
retain their stderr. Forced SIGKILL cannot perform a final leak scan.
`--helpers-only --token-mode` selects token-mode lifecycle coverage; omit
`--token-mode` for fuzzy coverage. Use a corrected baseline for full sanitized
GUI comparisons: the original pre-fix baseline still overreads during filtering.

Termination observation uses an earlier conservative bound (key injection start,
or helper startup before completion/timeout), never observer entry. Driver
delivery time is included, so the two-second budget is stricter than a budget
from the actual SIGKILL syscall. Late scans fail rather than establish absence
retroactively. Stale-frame sampling records its maximum gap; shorter flashes
remain outside the observation resolution. Fairness tests reject failed/slow
injectors or X-server round trips as infrastructure errors.


Fuzzy matching regression check
-------------------------------
Use a disposable source copy with the intended configuration. From a fresh
checkout, `make config.h` is required before compiling this standalone probe;
it preserves an existing local header rather than overwriting it:

    make config.h
    cc -std=c99 -pedantic -Wall -g -O1 -fno-omit-frame-pointer \
        -fsanitize=address,undefined -D_DEFAULT_SOURCE -D_XOPEN_SOURCE=700 \
        -DVERSION='"5.4"' -DXINERAMA -I/usr/include/freetype2 \
        tests/fuzzymatch.c drw.c util.c \
        -lX11 -lXinerama -lfontconfig -lXft -lm -o /tmp/fuzzymatch-check
    ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
        /tmp/fuzzymatch-check
    ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
        /tmp/fuzzymatch-check a a

Keep sanitizer options during both compilation and linking; do not define
NDEBUG, since it disables assertions. With no arguments, the probe asserts
unequal fuzzy scores and the exact expected order for `ab`, `zzab`, `axb`.
The second invocation is the original single-match overflow reproducer.
Optional arguments are `-i`, `-F`, a query, and item labels; `--` ends options.
The probe includes production matching code and checks both linked-list
directions without opening X11 or initializing Fontconfig, so it can retain
leak detection. It is not evidence of GUI correctness. For actual X11 sanitizer
runs only, use the focused leak setting above and report leak-enabled exits
separately; never suppress the original overflow or other invalid accesses.
